This guide adds service-private background jobs to the Rust `orders-service` from
[Tutorial: Write a service](/guides/write-a-service). For caller-visible async
workflows, use [Operations: Rust](/guides/using-operations-rust).

## 1. Declare queues

Declare each queue in the service contract. Payload, result, and optional update
schemas are part of the generated Rust API:

```json
{
  "jobs": {
    "reserveInventory": {
      "payload": { "schema": "ReserveInventoryPayload" },
      "result": { "schema": "ReserveInventoryResult" },
      "maxDeliver": 3,
      "backoffMs": [1000, 5000, 15000]
    }
  }
}
```

Run `cargo xtask install`. The participant facade now contains a typed queue
registration method and a cloneable submission client.

## 2. Keep a submission client

Clone submission clients before registering handlers. They can be moved into RPC
handlers, operation providers, or background tasks without borrowing the
connected service:

```rust
let reserve_inventory = service.jobs_client().reserve_inventory();
let reserve_inventory_for_create = reserve_inventory.clone();

service.handle().rpc().orders().create(move |_context, request| {
    let reserve_inventory = reserve_inventory_for_create.clone();
    async move {
        let order_id = ulid::Ulid::new().to_string();

        let job = reserve_inventory
            .submit(orders_sdk::ReserveInventoryJobPayload {
                order_id: order_id.clone(),
                items: request.items,
            })
            .await?;

        Ok(orders_sdk::OrdersCreateResponse {
            order_id,
            job_id: job.identity().id.clone(),
        })
    }
});
```

`submit(...)` returns a typed `JobRef`. Use `get()`, `wait()`, or `cancel()` on
that reference; do not query private job subjects.

## 3. Register workers

Register one handler for each queue before `service.run()`:

```rust
use trellis_rs::jobs::JobProgress;

service
    .jobs()
    .reserve_inventory()
    .handle(|job| async move {
        tracing::info!(
            request_id = %job.context().request_id,
            attempt = job.tries(),
            "reserving inventory",
        );

        job.progress(JobProgress {
            step: Some("reserving".into()),
            message: Some("Checking inventory".into()),
            ..Default::default()
        })
        .await
        .map_err(|error| error.to_string())?;

        if job.is_cancelled() {
            return Err("job cancelled".to_string());
        }

        reserve_items(&job.payload().items).await
            .map_err(|error| error.to_string())?;

        Ok(orders_sdk::ReserveInventoryJobResult { reserved: true })
    })
    .await?;
```

The payload and result types are inferred from the generated queue descriptor.
Handler errors implement `ToString`; returning an error requests redelivery until
the queue reaches `maxDeliver`, after which the job becomes dead.

## 4. Run and supervise workers

`service.run().await` starts the authenticated request host and every registered
worker. There is no separate worker host to retain. Unexpected worker exit fails
the service and cancels sibling runtime tasks.

For work that can exceed the queue's ack wait, call `job.heartbeat().await?`
periodically. Heartbeats extend delivery ownership; worker-presence heartbeats
are separate Trellis runtime traffic and require no application code.

## 5. Cancellation and redelivery

Cancellation is cooperative. Check `job.is_cancelled()` at safe interruption
points. Use `job.is_redelivery()` and `job.redelivery_count()` when behavior must
change on a retry.

Per-job processing failures NAK the message and keep the queue worker alive.
Keyed queues release their active slot on a best-effort basis before redelivery.

## 6. Progress, logs, and updates

Use `job.progress(...)` for durable, low-frequency lifecycle progress and
`job.log(...)` for operator-visible log entries. If the queue declares an update
schema, typed live updates are at most once: they are not persisted, replayed,
shown in Jobs admin, or delivered to late subscribers. Prefer cumulative update
payloads and treat the terminal result as authoritative.

## 7. Keyed queues

Use `keyConcurrency` when only one job for a logical business key may run at a
time. Keyed queues default to clear failure (`fail-stale`, `reject`); choose
coalescing or replacement explicitly when that is the domain behavior.

The generated facade initializes the Trellis key coordinator automatically from
the resolved queue binding. Service code does not construct a `JobManager` or
coordinator.

